      "extracellular": true,
      "functional_impact_measured": "IP accumulation assay (L8): pEC50 for Ca2+ of 2.50 \u00b1 0.02 (FLAG-SNAP-CaSR WT) vs 2.39 \u00b1 0.03 (HA-CaSR WT) \u2014 no significant difference, confirming Gq/11 pathway activation is preserved. Surface expression confirmed by ELISA (L7) and FACS on intact cells (L13, L14).",
      "gene_symbol": "CASR",
      "insert_after_residue": 19,
      "library": null,
      "linker": null,
      "loop_run_position": null,
      "median_conservation": null,
      "numbered_against": null,
      "plddt": null,
      "position_evidence": "topology_derived",
      "position_frame": null,
      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "rationale": "Multiple papers report N-terminal tags (FLAG-SNAP, HA) on full-length human CaSR that are accessible on the extracellular face of intact cells. CaSR has a signal peptide (residues 1\u201319) and a large extracellular N-terminus beginning at residue 20; the N-terminal tag must be placed after signal peptide cleavage (insert_after_residue = 19, residue_before = A, residue_after = Y). No residue is named in any clip, so the junction is topology-derived: the mature N-terminus is the sole extracellular terminus available. L8 demonstrates function is fully preserved (Ca2+-induced IP accumulation indistinguishable from HA-CaSR WT); L13/L14 confirm surface accessibility by FACS on intact cells. Full-length human CaSR with N-terminal FLAG-SNAP tag (L7\u2013L9, PMID 31399503) and N-terminal HA tag (L13\u2013L14, DOI 10.1101/2024.07.08.602375) expressed in HEK293 cells; surface display confirmed by ELISA (L7) and FACS of intact non-permeabilized cells with PE-conjugated anti-HA antibody (L14). No specific residue is named; the junction is derived from the signal peptide cleavage site (residues 1\u201319) leaving the mature N-terminus at residue 20.",
      "residue_after": "Y",
      "residue_before": "A",
      "residue_label": "A19",
      "residue_range": null,
      "site_id": "CASR-terminal_n-19-lit",
      "site_kind": "terminal_n",
      "source_tier": "paper",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 31399503,
          "citation": "PMID 31399503",
          "claim": "In a functional IP accumulation assay there was no difference in agonist potency of Ca 2 1 for thetwo receptor constructs (Fig. 1A;pEC values of 2.50 6 0.02 and 2.39 6 0.03 for FLAG-SNAP-CaSR WT and HACaSR WT, respectively), thereby confirming that the SNAP tag did not prevent Ca 2 1 -mediated activation of the G q/11 pathway.",
          "title": "Calcium-Sensing Receptor Internalization Is \u03b2-Arrestin-Dependent and Modulated by Allosteric Ligands",
          "authors": "Mos et al.",
          "journal": "Mol Pharmacol",
          "year": 2019
        }
